Reaching certain degrees of automation (cf., SAE level 3–5 [22]), the system can complete the driving task itself, at least for a certain time, depending on the level. From then on, the human driver becomes the passenger (e.g., from entering until exiting the highway) and is free to engage in non-driving-related activities (abbr.: NDRAs): one can do work, entertainment, or social activities during travel. Nevertheless, even in autonomous driving phases, humanvehicle interaction is still necessary to provide mechanisms to the human driver to intervene in the driving task, e.g., to adapt driving parameters, take cooperative maneuvers, or foster a sense of control. The NDRAs highly influence the context of such drivingrelated activities (abbr.: DRAs). When the occupants want to intervene in the driving process, they must shift their attention to the new task, and the current activity must be interrupted. Interference between NDRA and DRA because of similar requirements for spatial/verbal cognitive resources (cf. [25]), might lead to performance breakdowns. Thus, it is crucial to understand the interplay between NDRA and DRAs in automated vehicles. While the definition [19], prediction [5, 12, 13, 18], and automated recognition [17] of NDRAs, as well as their influence on take-over request performance [6, 10, 15, 20, 27] have been studied recently, their influence in less critical multitasking scenarios is still unexplored. This paper therefore explores the influence of highly anticipated non-driving-related activities (main task) on the driver/passenger’s modality choices when intervening in the driving process (side task) in a driving simulator experiment (N = 20). To interact in a subjectively natural and comfortable way, participants could freely choose between touch, voice, and mid-air gesture modalities. We were interested in resulting patterns of interaction and possible differences in multitasking performance. Thereby, we hypothesize that (1) NDRAs would influence the chosen input modalities for control interventions, and (2) the selected modality would be different from the modality used for the main task. Our study helps to understand better which interaction modalities are used naturally in the context of different NDRAs. Further, it is a first step towards the activity-optimized development of in-car HMIs for driving-related control interventions in future automated cars.
2 RELATED WORK
In the following, we distinguish DRAs from NDRAs and show how control in automated cars will change. The growing automation will lead to new activities, and new activities will change the user requirements for in-car interfaces.
2.1 Non-Driving-Related Activities
In conventional cars, one can divide the driving task into three categories (cf. Bubb [1]): 1) transportation-related tasks which are


