4.4 Overall Preference for a Modality
Participants subjective rating for voice (M = 4.30, SD = 0.73), touch (M = 4.00, SD = 1.26), and mid-air gesture (M = 3.70, SD = 1.22) input modalities after the experiment is high. A repeated-measures ANOVA (sphericity checked with Mauchly’s test, p > .05) reveals no significant main effect of the modality on rating (F (2, 38) = 1.81, p = .18, η2 = .09). Hence, there is no clear preference for a particular modality yet visual inspection shows a non-significant trend with voice being higher rated than touch and touch higher than mid-air gestures.
5 DISCUSSION
In the following, we discuss our findings and point out limitations and directions for future work on multitasking during autonomous driving. In this experiment, we did not control the modality switches but gave participants a choice. This kind of procedure allows for an economic sample size and an analysis of switching preferences/patterns. It might be useful for exploring multitasking with multimodal interfaces in other contexts, too. However, a more extensive follow-up


