study could investigate modality switches in fixed pairs. We hypothesized that (1) NDRAs would influence the chosen input modalities for control interventions, and (2) the selected modality would be different from the modality used for the main task. We could fully confirm the first and partly confirm the second assumption with our experiment. In terms of multitasking modality switch patterns, we saw that participants use more than one interaction modality per condition (change ratio > .22 / flexibility > .24). These modality changes could be explained by the findings of Roider et al. [21] that transitions between interaction modalities can increase efficiency compared to unimodal conditions. Further, we observed that the in terms of overall frequency, Eating (chewing) and Music (acoustic interference) conditions lead to comparably less voice interaction, while for the Smartphone (manual demand) and Conversation (supporting gestures) conditions, mid-air gesture input is less used. These findings indicate that users tend to avoid resource conflicts through an NDRA occupied modality as input for the control intervention, which is in line with the second hypothesis. Thus, future vehicles should provide redundant control mechanisms to the users because they will likely perform various NDRAs during a trip. Through tracking the occupants’ activities, the system could promote the most feasible modality choice. Future work could also investigate the specific promotion of side-task interaction (control interventions) for common NDRAs and develop strategies to train users to make sure they use each interaction modality’s full potential from the beginning on, e.g., through User Onboarding. We observed a high increase of touch interaction during the smartphone condition, which is, in contrast, not in line with the second assumption. In terms of overall multitasking load, we found the smartphone condition to be different from the baseline. This could be explained by the multiple resources theory because the smartphone condition had a high combined spatial (typing/watching) and verbal (typing/listening) demand on all processing stages. Given
that smartphone use is the most anticipated NDRA in automated vehicles, it will be challenging to design systems in a way that shifts the users’ attention and facilitate the switch to other tasks. Future concepts thus might integrate the use of the smartphone in multitasking procedures (cf. Wintersberger et al. [27]). Given the higher total multitasking workload in the smartphone condition, we assume that users have less free cognitive resources to use nonfamiliar interaction styles and, therefore, instead stick to known patterns. Touch interaction, known from everyday smartphone use, might feel more comfortable. This bias would also explain the general tendency of using touch interaction, despite the mid-ranking in the users’ overall modality preferences. Future work could pick up this challenge look into the task switching efficiency, as an already demanding modality might decrease interaction efficiency (cf. Roider et al. [21]). Beyond the previously discussed resource conflicts, other factors like social norms might influence the interaction behavior, e.g., eating while talking to somebody or something might be considered rude behavior, or using an “expressive” interaction style like mid-air gesture or voice interaction might be socially inhibited.
5.1 Limitations of Study
In our study, we used a WoZ system. The error rate of voice and mid-air gesture recognition systems will be higher for a real implementation, and findings might differ. Our sample was relatively small and homogeneous in this initial study, consisting mainly of young male students with a strong technical background. Future work should investigate a larger and more heterogeneous user sample to strengthen the generalizability of the findings.
6 CONCLUSION
During autonomous driving, vehicle occupants will perform NDRAs, and, sometimes, intervene in the driving process. The former relation of main and side tasks will be reversed. Driving will become an


CHI ’21 Extended Abstracts, May 8–13, 2021, Yokohama, Japan Detjen, Geisler & Schneegass
optional side task. Our study shows that NDRAs lead to different interaction modality choices while multitasking in the car, highlighting the importance of multimodal interaction design. For an intervention, users tend to avoid resource conflicts with an occupied modality, e.g., voice interaction becomes less frequent while eating or listen to music, and mid-air gestures become less frequent during conversations or smartphone use. In contrast, when the multitasking workload is comparably high, users tend to stick to an interaction style, as we found for touch interaction during smartphone use. Overall, we recommend implementing control concepts that rely on multimodal interfaces to account for future autonomous vehicles’ diverse situations and activities. Despite the potential for more research on multitasking support in the vehicle, be it for car or entertainment control, our findings help sensitize the designers to the NDRA context of interaction when planning new vehicle interiors and interfaces.
